  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish-queries> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/EvidenceItem> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish-queries> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Ontology queries and term identifiers behind the \"a flying-fish with enlarged pectoral fins that glides above the water\" blueprint" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish-queries> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "12 SPARQL queries against Ubergraph (Uberon, Cell Ontology, Protein Ontology and NCBI Taxonomy with materialised subClassOf and part_of closures). Each query is a part of this evidence item below, carrying its purpose, its full query text, the endpoint, the retrieval time, the row count, and two SHA-256 hashes: sparql-sha256 is the hash of the query text, which is also the name of the file holding the response under organism-design/cache/, and response-sha256 is the hash of that file. A clean checkout replays the same bytes offline and can confirm it did. Latest retrieval 2026-10-31T15:42:53.199Z. The 82 body, organ-system and organ identifiers of this blueprint are referenced below; the 198 tissue and 257 cell-type identifiers are listed in the cited file." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish-queries> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "organism-design/examples/a-flying-fish.json" .
